A1. A big turnover in the show business. ~ SOMERSAULT
A10. What that cute little baby next door can do. ~ BAWL
A14. This usually makes the first page. ~ CARTOON
A15. If you put your capital in this at first the result will be surprising. ~ OH
A16. This always comes back with the last word. ~ ECHO
A17. This is always near. ~ AT
A18. "And another thing." ~ ALSO
A19. Which arguments does one like to listen to—wet or dry? ~ NEITHER
A21. A little article Calvin Coolidge will have to use when he writes his 500-word history. ~ THE
A23. The cigarette a blindfolded Scotchman will choose. ~ LONGEST
A25. A labor measure. ~ ERG
A26. This might remind you of your Red Cross dues (abbr.). ~ RC
A28. A Swedish cognomen. ~ OLE
A29. This goes into seventeen twice. ~ EN
A30. Snarls. ~ GNARS
A33. This is cunning. ~ SLY
A34. Kings and queens often ride in this. ~ STATE
A37. What the president's cabinet is made up of. ~ ROSEWOOD
A39. This was put in to stop the flow of liquor. ~ SPIGOT
A40. A little wild thing that grew up without any culture. ~ DANDELION
A42. This is quite small in Paris. ~ PETITE
A45. What a woman gets out of a cigarette. ~ NICOTINE
A48. After you onee get into a circus it costs you nothing to go through these. ~ EXITS
A49. A prefix meaning before. ~ PRE
A50. Whose music was heard during a hot time in the old town? ~ NEROS
A51. A short order of fricasseed penguin. ~ FP
A52. The father of the cubs. ~ LEO
A53. The east side of Toledo. ~ DO
A54. This is a knock. ~ RAP
A56. These often come between the spring and the fall. (Ask the Prince of Wales.) ~ HURDLES
A60. This is very small—perhaps it has some Scotch in it. ~ NIP
A63. This is what makes the doorman a prince of a fellow. ~ UNIFORM
A65. This is between the surface lines. ~ AREA
A67. The west side of Toledo. ~ TO
A68. An all-around wet. ~ ISLE
A69. You might get a little kick out of this. (At least you might get a good start.) ~ KI
A70. Struck a snag. ~ SNAGGED
A72. This is what he did when he started playing golf. ~ TEED
A73. These were quiet, reserved and steady at first but later they began to have their ups and downs. ~ STATEROOMS

D1. A catty thing to say. ~ SCAT
D2. This is often heard on the golf links. ~ OATH
D3. What they call the man about town. ~ MR
D4. This is all Greek to us. ~ ETA
D5. This is always crusty before it gets its coffee. ~ ROLL
D6. Just tolerable. ~ SOSO
D7. Presently. ~ ANON
D8. The way she felt after she ate onions for lunch. ~ LONELY
D9. More than this. ~ THESE
D10. In Kentucky they often put this on a horse. ~ BET
D11. This is what the farmer expects from his corn next season. ~ ACHE
D12. An adverb a taxicab driver often uses. ~ WHERETO
D13. These were held up in Chicago right in front of the eyes of several opera-goers. ~ LORGNETTES
D20. That indescribable something. ~ IT
D22. It takes years to make these. ~ ERAS
D24. This little sneeze need not cry, It's going to be a balloon tire by and by. ~ GOLDENROD
D27. What Chicago has been looking for. ~ CREDIT
D30. This was put on the market and after it was split, it took a sudden spurt. (Better than cutting a melon.) ~ GRAPEFRUIT
D31. This starts noiselessly. ~ NO
D32. What Babe Ruth made before he was offered $80,000. ~ SWATS
D33. This covers many mistakes. ~ SOD
D34. This just goes along from hand to mouth. ~ SPOON
D35. After they did this things seemed to look brighter. ~ TINTED
D36. This is in again. ~ AG
D38. It is hard to make a hole in this. ~ ONE
D39. It was thus in Cæsar's day. ~ SIC
D41. What that cherry-tree story may be. ~ LIE
D43. What is between us and the new planet. ~ EXPANSE
D44. The way it looks in reverse. ~ TI
D46. An element of miner importance. ~ IRON
D47. The beginning of nothing. ~ NO
D49. This might get you into something. ~ PERMIT
D52. Hangs around. ~ LURKS
D55. This was made from the first Ford cars. ~ PILE
D56. The beginning of hope. ~ HO
D57. The kind of a president Herbert Hoover is as compared with the first president. ~ LAST
D58. A sea eagle. ~ ERNE
D59. Dry up. ~ SEAR
D61. A small subdivision. ~ ITEM
D62. These are always hanging around the garden. ~ PODS
D64. What the farmers have done for the nation. ~ FED
D66. You will find this after a long time. ~ AGO
D71. A sign of progression. ~ GO


